{"id": "16564", "revid": "1668123", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16564", "title": "Jurassic Coast", "text": "The Jurassic Coast is the popular (and now official) name given to a 95 mile (155 km) long stretch of coastline in southern England, incorporating parts of the east Devon and Dorset coasts.\nThe Jurassic Coast was inscribed as a World Heritage site in 2001 on account of its importance to geology and palaeontology, and the sheer beauty of its landscape. UNESCO lists it as Dorset and East Devon Coast. The area attracts hundreds of thousands of fossil hunters, hikers, campers and beach-goers each year.\nUnderstand.\nMany of the earliest recognised scientific dinosaur discoveries were made along the Jurassic Coast in the early 19th century, promoting the rise of the science of paleontology. Pioneering fossil hunters like Mary Anning took advantage of the coast's highly fossiliferous cliffs.\nGet in.\nThere is a South Western Railways service from London Waterloo to Poole and Exeter.\nGet around.\nThese bus services are of particular use to people visiting the Jurassic Coast:\nThe X53/X51[dead link] bus service from Axminster to Weymouth/Dorchester via Lyme Regis, Charmouth, Bridport, West Bay.\nThe 885 bus service from Axminster to Beer via Colyton and Seaton.\nThe 899 bus service from Seaton to Sidmouth via Beer and Branscombe. This bus route is very picturesque as it winds its way down the charming Devon lanes.\nThe X54[dead link] bus service from Weymouth to Poole via Wearham.\nThe 40 bus service from Swanage to Poole via Corfe Castle and Wearham.\nSee.\nThe attractions of the Jurassic Coast are many. Not to be missed, however, are:\nStay safe.\nCliffs can be prone to rockfalls, so avoid walking directly beneath them. This is not an abstract risk: people are killed this way with regrettable frequency."}
{"id": "16566", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16566", "title": "Jurata", "text": "Jurata is a village and a resort on Baltic Sea in Pomorskie Voivodship in Poland.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nSee PKP \nGet around.\nThe resort is quite small and has a very specific pine forest character. You can easily walk across the whole village on foot or rent a bicycle. Prior to 1928 it was a fishing village. Jurata is also the name of a Slavic pagan goddess of the Baltic Sea. The village was named after her.\nDo.\nAs a primarily beach village, visitors will spend most of their time sun-bathing and swimming. While the water is of excellent quality, it is cold at times, and visitors should watch for small jellyfish. Jurata also has excellent wind-surfing opportunities - renting, lessons, etc.\nEat.\nJurata is known for its amazing seafood - all varieties, in all forms. Visitors can also sample traditional Polish crepes and cabbage rolls, while more Western food is available as well.\nSleep.\nPrimarily nice, clean, and quaint small villas and apartment-style rooms, though Western resorts are gradually being built.\nGo next.\nGda\u0144sk Bay:\nVistula Bay:\nKashubian Coast:\nOn the Hel Peninsula:\nOn the Vistula Peninsula:"}
{"id": "16567", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16567", "title": "Jurien Bay", "text": "Jurien Bay is a coastal town 220 km north of Perth in the Wheatbelt region of Western Australia. In 2021 the population was 1985. \nThis coastline was mapped in 1801 by Nicolas Baudin, who named the spot for Charles Marie Jurien, a French navy bigwig. A sheep-farming town (little more than a few shacks) appeared in the 1850s, the first jetty was built, and Jurien Bay morphed into a fishing village. Better roads from the 1950s enabled it to grow, specialising in crayfish (Western Rock Lobster, \"Panulirus cygnus\"), and the airstrip from the 1990s facilitated their rapid global export. This is still a busy trade in town, but being close to the big city Jurien Bay has also become a holiday resort.\nGet in.\nJurien Bay is a 2\u00bd hr drive from Perth. The quickest route is by the coastal Indian Ocean Drive past Lancelin and Cervantes. You could also take Brand Hwy to the turn off at Jurien Bay Rd.\nIntegrity Coaches run three evenings a week from Perth, taking 3 hours via Joondalup, Lancelin and Cervantes. \n is the stop in Jurien Bay. The buses have a 30 min rest stop then head off into the night to Green Head, Leman, Dongara, Geraldton, Carnarvon, Coral Bay, Exmouth, Port Hedland and Broome.\nTranswa buses run inland on Brand Hwy. They can drop and pick-up at the turn off, but you're better taking the Integrity Coach to connect with their services, for instance in Geraldton for Kalbarri.\nGet around.\nThe town is small enough to walk around but most sights are at some distance. You need your own wheels with 4WD for these.\nConnect.\nAs of Jan 2023, the town has 4G from Optus and Telstra, but patchy coverage on the approach roads and nothing from Vodafone. 5G has not reached this area."}
{"id": "16569", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16569", "title": "Jutiapa", "text": "Jutiapa is a city in eastern Guatemala.\nGet in.\nThere are direct buses to Jutiapa from Guatemala City.\nMost groups rent a \"bus\" (which is mostly a small van) that will pick you from the airport, and bring you and your group to where you need to go.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nTraveling here, from town to town is relatively easy, but while road signs may be posted, and rules do apply, most locals note that if you want to successfully get around the city, you have to drive as if there were no rules.\nIn the towns, there are also taxis, or \"tuc-tuc's\" that cost 2 quetzales per person, and will take you where you need to go in the city. \nA street in Asuncion Mita, Jutiapa, with a red tuc-tuc to the right.\nBy bus.\nThere are buses that take crowds to farther areas of the town, but your best bet is to hitch a ride, or hail a tuc-tuc\nWork.\nMost that live in Guatemala, possess some type of skill or trade that they learn either in high school, or from a mentor, which becomes their area of specialty. \nMany families open up small shops that cater to their neighborhood.\nBuy.\nOn just about every street is a small \"tienda\" (store) that caters to the surrounding neighbors, and carries the items that you \"just ran out of\", or need in case of emergency. Otherwise you can hit the crowded streets of the marqueta (market place) where vendors sell clothes, fabric, fruits, and items in bulk.\nHere, unless there are marked prices, bargaining is a way of life.\nEat.\nBudget.\nIf you are staying with a family, a budget meal would be a simple, traditional, hand-cooked meal. Usually Tortillas, Juice or Soda, a kind of soup with meat, or beef/chicken, rice, and/or black beans.\nMid-range.\nMid-range spending would be done at local restaurants or fast food restaurants, which could be considered about the same.\nSplurge.\nThis could probably be done at restaurants that cater mostly to tourists, or the rare very high-class restaurants that may be in a city.\nSleep.\nThere is at least one hotel in or around each city. They are pretty affordable, though they are not always quite what Western/American nationals are used to; they usually are not air-conditioned, and come with sheets, a pillow, electricity, running water, and a towel. \nIn the areas that mostly cater to tourists, there is a mixture of upper and middle class hotels, that are much more expensive, but nicer.\nGo next.\nGo to El Ovejero because it is like Jutiapa."}
{"id": "16570", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16570", "title": "Jutland", "text": "Jutland (Danish: \"Jylland\") forms the mainland part of Denmark. It is a large peninsula on the European continent that stretch northwards towards the even larger Scandinavian Peninsula. \nThe entire Jutland peninsula is about 535 km long, originating around the small town of Lauenburg on the Elbe River in Northern Germany, stretching north all the way to the sandy spit of Skagens Odde in Denmark. Geographically, the southern third of the peninsula belongs to Germany, comprising the state of Schleswig-Holstein, and the northern two-thirds belongs to Denmark. This article will only deal with the Danish parts of Jutland. \nJutland makes up nearly 70% of Denmark's land area and is home to 2.7 million people, roughly half the country's population. Jutland's largest city is Aarhus, as Copenhagen, Denmark's capital and most populous city, is situated on the island of Zealand in the east.\nRegions.\nThe Danish parts of the Jutland peninsula, that this article deals with, comprise these main cultural regions:\nFor the German part of the Jutland peninsula, see:\nCities and towns.\nMajor cities.\nOrdered by size, some important major cities in Jutland are:\nOther towns of interest.\nThere are a lot of small picturesque towns throughout Jutland, and other towns of interest. This includes:\nIslands and nature.\nDenmark is an island nation with hundreds of small and large islands throughout, and Jutland is no different. The main islands of interest to tourists are these:\nMost of this region is actually an island in itself, seperated from the southern parts by the Limfjord strait, but in everyday terms, it is seldom regarded as such. With that in mind, the largest and most interesting islands in North Jutland are:\nNational parks.\nDenmark has five national parks, and four of them are in Jutland: \nIn addition to the large national parks, there are a plethora of smaller protected nature sites throughout and 7 nature parks (out of 14) are located in Jutland as well. Nature parks include cultural and historic areas in addition to nature specifically.\nUnderstand.\nThe German portion of the Jutland peninsula is historically a large borderland area between Denmark and Germany. Nationality and ownership has changed over the centuries, sometimes it was part of Denmark, sometimes it was part of Germany, depending on who had the upper hand in military terms. After the end of World War I, the current national borders were settled once and for all by a democratic referendum in 1920, involving the actual citizens in the Schleswig-Holstein region. Because of the shifting and muddled cultural influence, the Danish language, and Danish identity, is officially recognised by Germany in the region today, and many large towns in Schleswig-Holstein display excellent examples of historic Danish architecture, and cultural monuments.\nSeveral islands off the coast of Jutland, including the islands of R\u00f8m\u00f8, Sams\u00f8, and L\u00e6s\u00f8, are viewed as regionally part of Jutland, even though they are separated from the peninsula in geological terms.\nThe northernmost part of Northern Jutland is an island, separated from the rest of the peninsula by the Limfjord sea channel since a local flood in 1825, but in geographical terms, it is usually viewed as part of the peninsula nevertheless.\nAdministrative regions.\nThe four Jutland regions that Wikivoyage deals with are cultural and historical regions, and their boundaries are not well-defined. Administratively, the Danish part of Jutland has been divided in to these three regions since 2007: \nTalk.\nThe local language is Danish, which in some parts of Jutland is spoken with a distinct dialect.\nWhile English speakers are perhaps less prevalent than in Copenhagen, most people under 60 will still have at least some understanding of the language. \nDue to the borderland issues, German language proficiency is far better in South Jutland, than elsewhere in Denmark. German is also recognised as a language and identity for the German minority in this region, mirroring the same approach by Germany in Schleswig-Holstein, and everyone can be considered bi-lingual. German proficiency is also present along the entire Jutland West coast, to some degree, as this region is popular with German visitors in the summer.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nBillund Airport is the main airport on the peninsula, and number two in Denmark as a whole. It receives many international flights, and has direct connections with almost every major North European hub. Aalborg and Aarhus airports also see substantial traffic, with a few international routes. There are also several smaller regional airports dotted around Jutland with a couple of daily flights from Copenhagen.\nBy train.\nJutland is well connected to mainland Europe by rail from Germany, including night train. The rest of Scandinavia (Norway and Sweden) can reach Jutland by train across the \u00d8resund Bridge at Copenhagen, but with the exception of East Denmark and Southern Sweden, this is a really time consuming mode of travel.\nBy car.\nThere are two ways to get into Jutland by car.\nBy bus.\nJutland can be easily reached by bus from Funen, and Copenhagen.\nThere are many international Flixbus routes to Jutland from the rest of Europe. Unless you come from Germany, Poland, Netherlands, or southern Sweden this is a time consuming mode of travel.\nBy boat.\nThere are several ferry lines connecting to Jutland from southern Norway and the Swedish west coast (see Hirtshals, Frederikshavn, and Gren\u00e5). \nFrom the rest of the Nordic Region, the weekly ferries from Iceland and the Faroe Islands to mainland Europe arrives at Hirtshals. \nDomestically, Jutland can be reached by ferry from Zealand, Funen, and Lolland, and for the latter, this is the fastest way to get to Jutland.\nGet around.\nBy train.\nAll major towns and cities in Jutland can be reached by train. The \"Lilleb\u00e6ltsbroen\" bridge to the island of Funen, connects Jutland to the rest of Denmark. Train tickets can also by used on local buses.\nBy bus.\nFor intercity transportation, there is a dense network of regional buses. Prices are set according to distance, as a rule of thumb (while in no way completely accurate) expect to pay around /km.\nAll cities and larger towns have their own local bus services.\nBy other transportation.\nFerry transport is used to reach Jutland's islands, except for R\u00f8m\u00f8, Mand\u00f8, and Als, that is reached by road exclusively. \nAarhus has had an electric lightrail system since 2017 that connects to nearby towns Odder and Gren\u00e5 as well.\nIt is possible to fly between Jutland's three international airports in Billund, Aalborg, and Aarhus, but this way of locomotion is seldom preferred.\nSee.\nIn addition to cities, towns and nature, Jutland has many cultural and historic sites of interest, and these are among the most interesting to tourists:\nNorth Jutland.\nAalborg is the old historic urban centre of North Jutland. Beautiful, old historic town centre and plenty of culture, history, and entertainment around town. \nVoergaard Castle is a beautiful 16th-century renaissance castle in the countryside north of Dronninglund. It is now a public museum.\nSkagen is a small but old, and artsy, historic town on the top of Jutland with some unique surrounding nature. It includes R\u00e5bjerg Mile, one of the biggest migrating dunes in Europe.\nRubjerg Knude is a former lighthouse in L\u00f8kken, standing 60 m above sea in up to 30-m-high sand dunes. \nBeach hotels are numerous on the west coast in North Jutland; in fact this region have the highest concentration of beach hotels in the country.\nNorth Sea Oceanarium in the coastal town of Hirtshals, offers you a chance to experience nature of the North Sea without getting wet.\nHanstholm Bunkermuseum is a large World War II museum in an abandoned bunker in the west coast fishing town of Hanstholm.\nL\u00e6s\u00f8 is an island in the Kattegat with a relaxing atmosphere and beautiful protected nature.\nWest Jutland.\nThe entire west coast of the region is a neverending landscape of coastal dunes. The beaches are attractions in themselves, but special sights are worthy of mention, such as the Tirpitz Museum in Bl\u00e5vand, west of Varde. It is an underground museum carved into the westcoast dunes at the abandoned Tirpitz Bunker from World War II. Experience the bunker's history, and other themes tied to the west coast of Jutland.\nThe interior of West Jutland comprise dry sandy heathlands, a rough environment for a farming culture, but a beautiful sight for visitors when the heather is in bloom. Herning, the main town of the region, and an important transport hub, has a proud industrial history of textile production. A cultural highlight here, and for the entire region, is the HEART Herning Museum of Contemporary Art on the eastside of town, exhibiting prominent modern and contemporary art by Danish and international artists. The building sits in a cluster of other interesting art exhibits.\nIn contrast to the dry heathland, Skjern \u00c5 National Park comprise a large green river delta nature site in the south and the small town of Skjern is a gateway to this area.\nEast Jutland.\nEast Jutland is a fertile region with lush forests, lakes, and agricultural fields throughout. \nAarhus, sometimes referred to as \"the capital of Jutland\", is the largest city in Jutland and has plenty of culture, history, architecture, nature, city-life, and shopping to offer visitors. With a sizeable agricultural industry, it is also considered a great spot for foodies as well. The Aarhus University is the largest university in the Nordic countries, which attracts many young people, but it has a long history that is particularly evident in the city center and can be experienced in part at the Aarhus Domkirke, an 800-year-old, large medieval cathedral. The three largest and most popular museums in the city are the Old Town Museum, the Moesgaard Museum of archaelogy, and the ARoS art museum, and they are also national attractions.\nViborg, another historic cathedral town, also presents a beautiful preserved medieval town centre. Viborg Domkirke with its amazing frescoes, and the entire church quarter around it, is a highlight. The abandoned limestone mines M\u00f8nsted Kalkgruber (and Daubjerg Kalkgruber) west of town, also makes the list. \nHimmelbjerget is an important historical place for the founding of the modern democratic state of Denmark, and presents magnificent views from the top across the lush lake-highland region around Silkeborg. Guden\u00e5en, the biggest river in Denmark, emerges around here, and pass through the forests and a plethora of lakes, winding its way north to the town of Randers. \nThe Jelling stones near the town of Jelling are big runestones from the Viking Age, signifying the official founding of Denmark as a united kingdom and a Christian nation around 960 AD. The site also includes historic burial mounds and an engaging museum exhibition. One of eight UNESCO World Heritage Sites in Denmark.\nSouth Jutland.\nThe west coast of South Jutland has endless meadows and vast tidal flats. Vadehavscentret is the visitor centre for the Wadden Sea National Park area. Experience the Wadden Sea through the eyes of the migratory birds who depends on this special region. R\u00f8m\u00f8, Fan\u00f8, and the small Mand\u00f8 in between, are noteable islands here, with beautiful nature and amazing sandy beaches.\nBeautiful, historical towns and villages are dotted throughout the South Jutland region, including Ribe, T\u00f8nder, Christiansfeld, and Haderslev not the least. The history of the entire region, is a tale of cutural and national struggles with Germany, which can be experienced at Dybb\u00f8l Banke near the town of S\u00f8nderborg, and at the beautiful, hilly nature site Skamlingsbanken near the town of Kolding specifically.\nThe east coast of South Jutland features calm fjords and lush nature. S\u00f8nderborg, as the unofficial cultural capital of South Jutland, and the associated island of Als, boasts a series of cultural and historical attractions, including royal castles.\nDo.\nEntertainment.\nThere are several amusement parks in Jutland: F\u00e5rup Sommerland in Blokhus, Djurs Sommerland, west of Gren\u00e5, Lalandia, a large family-oriented waterworld with locations in Billund and S\u00f8ndervig, and Legoland in Billund.\nBeaches.\nWest coast beaches. Almost the entire western coastline of Jutland, from the Wadden Sea area in south to the Skagen spit in the north, presents amazing wide sandy beaches. In the autumn and winter, the weather and the wind from the North Sea can be rough and hard, but on sunny days in the summer, things are lovely and attracts a lot of visitors and beach-goers. Go to these spots to mingle:\nHiking.\nEven though Jutland, and all of Denmark really, is quite flat throughout, the nature and landscape is varied and presents several types of nature, each with their own charm. Hiking is a safe and pleasant activity in Jutland; there is no outright deadly animals or wildlife to look out for, really, and you are never far away from a town or settlement. A number of hiking routes highlights good nature experiences, and these are considered among the very best:\nGo next.\nDenmark is a gateway to the Nordic region, and in this respect, Jutland is a seafaring hub. From Jutland you can travel by ship to these Nordic destinations:"}
{"id": "16574", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16574", "title": "Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4", "text": "Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 is a lively university city and the capital of Central Finland. About a third of the city's 148,000 inhabitants (2023) are students. Beautifully set between lakes and ridges, many motorsports fans are familiar with the city as the Neste Oil Rally of the World Rally Championships series takes place around the city each July. The works of the notable Finnish architect Alvar Aalto can be seen throughout the city. \nUnderstand.\nElias L\u00f6nnrot, the compiler of the Finnish national epic, the \"Kalevala\", gave the city the nickname \"Athens of Finland\". This nickname refers to the major role of Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 as an educational centre: the \"seminar\" founded in 1863 was the first institution educating teachers in Finnish. The seminar later developed into a university, inaugurated in 1954. The city is home of the annual Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 Arts Festival.\nThere are 328 lakes in the municipality, and lakes and rivers constitute 20% of its total area. \nMost of today's Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 was built after the Continuation War, when evacuees from territories ceded to the Soviet Union were settled in the city, and housing was badly needed. \nGet in.\nBy car.\nJyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 is an important node in the Finnish highway network, and roads from many directions pass through it. These are the major ones:\nBy train.\nJyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 has mostly adequate rail connections via Tampere and via Pieks\u00e4m\u00e4ki, and a mostly daily railcar from Sein\u00e4joki. A normal train ticket between Helsinki and Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 for an adult costs \u20ac50\u2013100.\nThe (\"Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 travel centre\", or \"Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4n matkakeskus\" in Finnish) is open M\u2013Sa 06:00\u201322:00 and Su 08:00\u201322:00. There is no booking office; train tickets are sold at ticket machines at opening hours or you can buy the tickets online.\nBy bus.\nCoaches travel between Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 and other Finnish cities, as well as smaller destinations. Direct routes are available for example from Helsinki, Tampere, Turku, Lahti, Oulu, Kuopio, Lappeenranta, Mikkeli, Kajaani, Savonlinna, Joensuu and Kokkola. A ticket from Helsinki to Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 will usually cost about \u20ac10\u201350, and can usually be bought from the driver, except for Onnibus. Online is recommended. See Matkahuolto for schedules and prices. \nComing via Tallinn or Stockholm, there may be coach connections from the ports of Helsinki with arranged transfer, mostly via Kamppi. Coming via Turku harbour, there may be a direct service from the ferry terminal to Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4. If you come by plane, some services to Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 depart directly from Helsinki-Vantaa airport.\nBy plane.\n is a small airport 20 km from Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 centre. NyxAir flies from Helsinki. used by the Finnish air force with a base nearby, and their flight school. There are also some direct charter flights to popular holiday destinations.\nYou reach the city by taxi in 15\u201320 minutes. Calling a taxi from the airport is easy as there is a special Taxi Phone. A taxi will cost approx. \u20ac50\u201360 (2021) to get to the city centre.\nBy boat.\nP\u00e4ij\u00e4nne Cruises Hilden operates between Lahti and Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 and between Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 and Suolahti through the Keitele-P\u00e4ij\u00e4nne Canal.\nGet around.\nJyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 has invested in cycle and pedestrian routes. As the town centre is not big you might want to walk or cycle to see the sights. Almost 1/3 of the population of Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 are students who mainly use bicycles to get around in the central area. Roads are also in good condition. There are bicycle and car rentals in the city. \nBy bus.\nLocal buses travel between the different parts and suburbs of Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4. You can use route planner.\nSingle tickets are sold by the bus driver. The basic fare for adults is \u20ac3 and for children \u20ac1.50 per trip (2021). The night fare (23:00\u201304:00) is \u20ac1 extra, often making a taxi a cheaper option when travelling in groups of three or more. If you need to change buses during the trip you have to show your receipt in the next bus within one hour from buying the ticket. Waltti mobile app can be used for ticket purchase.\nMatkahuolto Routes and Tickets app is able to first plan the route and after it sell the right ticket for it.\nIf you plan to stay longer and want to use the bus service regularly, you might want to consider buying a bus card], which will be cheaper (much cheaper if you are a student living in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4). A basic day ticket costs \u20ac8/6/4 (2021). A 30-day bus card costs ca \u20ac60 (without reduction) and it can be used as much as wanted during this 30-day period. \nNo matter which part of Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 you want to get to, there are approximately three buses leaving each hour during the daytime. However, during the summer months from June to August the frequency is reduced to two buses per hour, in some more remote villages there are no buses at all. You can buy the tickets at the Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 information centre on Asemakatu in the city centre.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis can be usually be found waiting at the Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4n matkakeskus or other taxi poles in the centre of the city. If there are no taxis present, you have to call for one. The taxi service number in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 area is \u260f. The starting cost in daytime M\u2013Sa is \u20ac4 and at other times \u20ac7 (2021). The cost per travelled kilometre varies between \u20ac1.09 and \u20ac1.75 depending on the number of passengers and time of the day. The price list is always available in the taxis.\nBy electric kick scooter.\nGerman Tier, Swedish Voi and American Bird rent electric kick scooters for use in the centre. Tier has also electric bicycles.\nSee.\nMuseums.\nExhibitions and art museums:\nAdmission is free on winter Fridays to Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 Art Museum, Craft Museum of Finland and Museum of Central Finland. Ask for a student discount, though often a Finnish student card is necessary. The national Museum Card gives free admission in most museums.\nAlvar Aalto sites.\nJyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 has many buildings by the famous architect Alvar Aalto. These include kaupunginteatteri (City Theatre), some buildings in the main campus of the university and the Alvar Aalto Museum. The Aalto Campus, the experimental house in Muuratsalo and S\u00e4yn\u00e4tsalo town hall are part of the Aalto Works UNESCO World Heritage Site.\nNature trails.\nThere are several recreational 'nature paths' in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4. They are mainly situated in forest areas.\nDo.\nThe city's web site has an event calendar[dead link] for current and upcoming events.\nWinter activities.\nEquipment can be found at some second-hand shops and flea markets, in addition to sport shops. See Buy below. Equipment for hire in Laajavuori.\nSwimming and beaches.\nJyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 has plenty of lakes and these provide nice opportunities for swimming and general beach activities during the warm summer months. Some of them offer ice swimming during the winter.\nBuy.\nDuring the summer, the marketplace (Yliopistonkatu 15) is a good place to visit if you want fresh vegetables or fish, flowers or craft items.\nYou'll definitely want to look for shopping opportunities at the Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 pedestrian street Kauppakatu. \nSecond-hand bookshops.\nThere are three antiquarian book stores in Kauppakatu.\nFlea markets.\nThere is also a few flea markets worth visiting in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4.\nThe second-hand equipment for winter sports (ice-skates and skis) may be bought for example from SPR fleamarket in Palokka or Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 Sportti-Divari.\nEat.\nIt's cheap to have lunch at the student restaurants. They are scattered all over the university area. With a Finnish student card it costs about \u20ac2.6. Without the card it costs about \u20ac5.5. There are many pizzerias offering large pizzas for as cheap as \u20ac5, guaranteed to fill your belly.\nDrink.\nAlko shop (wine, strong beer and spirits) e.g. in the Sokos department store on Kauppakatu.\nPubs, Bars and Nightclubs.\nNightlife is centred in the downtown part of Kauppakatu and cross streets, some also in the uptown part of Kauppakatu, near the University. As most of the bars are situated on Kauppakatu or very close to it, pub crawls can be easily arranged in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4. Start from Yl\u00e4kaupunki and work your way down to the clubs in Alakaupunki!\nAlakaupunki (Downtown).\nA large number of bars, restaurants and nightclubs are located around the pedestrian district. Cheaper places in this area include Bar Explosive and Bar Passion. Suggested establishments:\nYl\u00e4kaupunki (Uptown).\nIf you prefer a more laid-back night scene than euro-disco hell, walk uphill along Kauppakatu until you are past the church. This is where Yl\u00e4kaupunki begins. Suggested establishments:\nSleep.\nThere are a few hotels in the vicinity of the train station. All the major hotel chains (Scandic, Cumulus, Sokos) can also be found in the city.\nConnect.\nThe city of Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 does not offer a free wifi as such. However, e.g. every library in the city has both computers with internet access free, and free wifi. Usually the wifi password is written on a sign somewhere but you can ask any librarian, as they all speak English. Many companies offer free wifi. \nRestaurants and bars also have wifi connections for customers, but a password is usually needed and must specifically be asked for.\nStay safe.\nThe crime rate in Jyv\u00e4skyl\u00e4 is generally low. However, it's best to avoid obviously drunk people during weekend nights after the bars close. Long queuing for taxis or late night snack sometimes frustrate some partygoers to seek fighting opponents. Sometimes this results to a temporary conversion from a pedestrian district to fighting arena, but the common sight of patrolling police makes the occurrences more rare. Despite this, walking on the streets at night (or any time of day) is safe.\nIn the winter time the streets might be slippery and that it is mandatory by traffic law to wear a reflector during the dark period."}
{"id": "16577", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16577", "title": "Ka'u", "text": "Ka'u is the largest district on the Big Island of Hawaii between Puna and Kona, consisting mostly of lava fields, grasslands and agricultural exports including Ka'u Coffee, macadamia nuts, and oranges. Volcanoes National Park lies both in the districts of Ka'u and Puna. \nSouth Point and Punalu'u Black Sand Beach are notable areas to travelers, as well as Kula Kai Caverns in Ocean View and the Punalu'u Bakeshop in Na'alehu.\nNaalehu is the southernmost town in the United States.\nGet in.\nHighway 11 is the only available highway to take you all the way through Ka'u from South Kona to Hilo.\nBuy.\nInteresting and cheap stuff at the Ocean View Swap Meet on Saturday Mornings."}
{"id": "16579", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16579", "title": "Kaafu", "text": "Kaafu is a region of the Maldives, which covers North Male Atoll, South Male Atoll and the islands of Kaashidhoo and Gaafaru.\nUnderstand.\nSurrounding both the capital Male and its airport, North and South Male have seen the heaviest amount of tourist development in the country. Practically all uninhabited islands of this atoll became tourist resorts during the two final decades of the 20th century; Club Med and Sheraton are two of the companies with resorts there.\nGet in.\nPractically all visitors fly into Velana International Airport, near the capital island Mal\u00e9. from where all resorts in Kaafu are accessible from Velana International Airport in an hour by speedboat or 15 minutes by Seaplane.\nDo.\nScuba diving and snorkelling are the main activities.\nSleep.\nAll resorts listed here are on private islands.\nGo next.\nMost resorts run shopping and sightseeing excursions to Male."}
{"id": "16581", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16581", "title": "Kaanapali", "text": "Kaanapali is a small town on the Island of Maui in Hawaii. It's located on Maui's Western shore, close to Lahaina, and is one of Maui's main tourist destinations.\nUnderstand.\nThe population was 1,250 at the 2010 census. The master-planned town is in the Old Hawaii ahupuaa of Hanaka\u02bb\u014d\u02bb\u014d, as in the same name of the southern end of Kaanapali Beach's Hanaka\u02bb\u014d\u02bb\u014d Canoe Beach.\nGet in.\nKa`anapali is about 10 minutes' drive by car from Lahaina as well as the Kapalua-West Maui Airport and about 45 minutes' drive from Kahului Airport, the main airport on the island, where the vast majority of travelers arrive. Route 30 is a road that connects Kaanapali with Lahaina and Kahului.\nMost major car rental companies have stations in north Ka`anapali (on Honoapi`ilani Hwy. between Ka`anapali Resort and Kapalua-West Maui Airport) and at the Kahului Airport terminal on Mokuea Pl. in Kahului.\nGet around.\nBy car.\nMost visitors to Maui rent a car. The budget constrained can take buses and shuttles and the adventurous can rent a geared bicycle.\nCars can be rented in Kahului Airport (all major rental companies have offices there) or in other locations throughout the island. Most hotels usually provide parking for their guests only, sometimes for an additional fee. Parking is available at the Whaler's Village shopping center, for a fee, but they will validate your ticket in stores and restaurants. If you go to one of the hotels for a luau, there is usually parking for the event, also for a fee in most cases.\nBy bus.\nThere is a shuttle between main Kaanapali hotels and Whaler's Village center. The local bus is $1 per ride. (You can take a Sugar Cane Train between Kaanapali and Lahaina, but this should be done for entertainment only.)\nBy bicycle.\nYou can rent a bicycle locally, or bring your own bicycle (folding bikes usually can be carried in a bag as luggage without attracting any extra charges). The main roads have paved shoulders marked as \"Bike Route\", and motorists are fairly relaxed.\nBy foot.\nYou can use a beautiful path along the beach to walk between major hotels.\nBuy.\nThere are a lot of shops and stores at "}
{"id": "16583", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16583", "title": "Kaarina", "text": "Kaarina (Swedish: \"Sankt Karins\u200e\") is a town in the Finland Proper region of Finland, about 10 km east of Turku.\nUnderstand.\nThe municipality is named after the Katariina church that in turn is named after Catherine of Alexandria (Saint Catherine of the Wheel).\nFrom a rural community, Kaarina has grown to 32,000 inhabitants and has been a town since 1993. Many of the inhabitants work in Turku. Though officially monolingual, Kaarina has a growing Swedish minority of about 5%, with its own primary school.\nThe former main village of Kaarina (Nummi) has been part of Turku since 1939. The present municipality of Kaarina has a few distinct centres. Kuusisto was annexed to Kaarina in 1946 and Piikki\u00f6 in 2009. Littoinen was the biggest settlement in the 1960s, with some industry and a train station (on the Turku\u2013Helsinki railway, now out of use). The modern centre is where the road to the archipelago (\"Saaristotie\", \"Sk\u00e4rg\u00e5rdsv\u00e4gen\") used to start, by the then main road to Helsinki.\nThe remains of a 12th-century church were found in Ravattula in 2013. This is the oldest known church in Finland. The Kaarina parish is mentioned 1309 and the wooden predecessor of the Katariina church was taken into use in 1351.\nGet in and around.\nKaarina is well connected to Turku. The former main road from Helsinki (now regional road 110) passes by Kaarina centre. The motorway from Helsinki (highway 1) also goes through Kaarina, but at a distance from the centre. Highway 10 (from H\u00e4meenlinna) passes through the northern parts of the municipality (by Auranlaakso). E18 deviates from highway 1 in Piikki\u00f6 and follows the Turku bypass (highway 40) to Naantali, passing by Auranlaakso and Ravattula in northern Kaarina.\nKaarinantie leads from highway 10 (H\u00e4meentie) in the north through Auranlaakso, west of Littoinen and over the Helsinki highways. It then becomes Saaristotie, which continues southwards, passing east of the centre and over the island Kuusisto, and leads to Pargas and farther out in the archipelago. The other main roads for getting around in Kaarina are highway 110 (Uudenmaantie) and Pyh\u00e4n Katariinan tie, the latter leading westward from the centre. \nThe Turku guide map includes Kaarina, with map layers for bus lines, biking routes etc.\nBy bus.\nMost coaches from Helsinki to Turku (even many express ones) leave the motorway in Kaarina and use road 110 for driving into Turku, stopping at \"Krossi\", \"Paraisten tienhaara\" and \"Piispanristi\", the former on Kaarinantie, the latter two on Uudenmaantie by Kaarina centre and by the border to Turku, respectively. See Matkahuolto.\nKaarina takes part in the Turku region \"F\u00f6li\" bus cooperation, which means bus tickets are treated as if Kaarina were part of the same municipality as Turku, Raisio, Naantali, Rusko and Lieto. Most regional buses (such as to Pargas, Kimito and Salo \u2013 look for line numbers or a \"F\u00f6li\" sign) can be used with the same tickets as long as you board and get off in the F\u00f6li area. A single ticket with two hours unlimited transfer costs about \u20ac4/2, see Turku#By bus.\nThe main bus lines from Turku to Kaarina centre are number 801 (Turku to Pargas) and 6A (from Naantali via Turku, with a detour to Koristo). Also e.g. lines 700, 701, 703\u2013708 and 720\u2013723 to Piikki\u00f6 pass by Kaarina centre and can be used in the same manner; line 701 continues to Toivonlinna of Piikki\u00f6, 703\u2013708 and 720\u2013723 continue to Paimio and some of them beyond. Lines 600 and 601 (?) go from Turku to Littoinen (use line 2, 3 or 4 to get to them from the centre), line 6 from Naantali via Turku along road 10 to Lieto. Internal buses (often minibuses) have a number prefixed by \"K\". Some of these latter zigzag by any residential areas on their way, so are good for a kind of sightseeing, but not very fast, and have limited schedules. K1 and K3 drive from Auranlaakso via Littoinen to Kaarina centre and K2 serves areas south of road 110, including Lemu and Kuusisto.\nThe F\u00f6li route planner is useful for getting around by bus in Kaarina.\nBy bike.\nKaarina has good bike routes mostly as needed. Distances are suitable for bike trips, but for getting around you have to budget quite some time, as the points of interest are spread around.\nThe map at kartta.turku.fi can show biking routes: open the layers menu in the upper left corner, choose Traffic, then Bicycle paths, and use the check boxes. Regional biking route 9 goes from Turku along H\u00e4meentie by Auranlaakso to Lieto, route 10 from Turku via Littoinen and Pyydysm\u00e4ki to Piikki\u00f6, route 11 along Uudenmaantie via Kaarina centre and Piikki\u00f6 to Paimio (Eurovelo 10 \"Baltic Sea\" probably follows this one), route 22 from Auranlaakso via Littoinen and Kaarina centre to Pargas, the Archipelago Trail (in the map: \"small ring route\") from Turku via Kaarina centre and Pargas farther out in the archipelago. At least these routes are well signposted.\nBuy.\nSome shopping in the centre. Large stores around the crossroads between highway 40 (Turku bypass, E18) and highway 10\nEat.\nThere are hamburgers, kebab, pizza and Chinese food available in Kaarina centre, and even a Nepali lunch restaurant. For fine dining, however, the common solution is to go to Turku. Nice dinners are available in some former manors and similar businesses (such as Villa Wolax, see Sleep below). For a nice lunch, also consider Tuorlan majatalo in Piikki\u00f6 4 km east of Kaarina centre.\nDrink.\nSome pubs and caf\u00e9s, most people go to Turku for nightlife."}
{"id": "16584", "revid": "2170049", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16584", "title": "Kabak", "text": "Kabak is a small village in Lycia on the Mediterranean coast of Turkey, 20 km south of \u00d6l\u00fcdeniz and 35 km south of Fethiye. Officially it's called Uzunyurt, which comprises Koza\u011fa\u00e7, Kirme, Hisar, Faralya (separately described), K\u0131z\u0131lcakaya and Kabak. The upper village is a scattering of \"pansiyons\" along the road, which deteriorates into a dirt lane and descends to the beach settlement or Kabak Koyu. Time was it had a hippy vibe and next-to-nothing prices, but nowadays, it can feel touristy, with prices to match. \nGet in.\nA narrow road winds 20 km from \u00d6l\u00fcdeniz, branching off the main highway from Fethiye near the Blue Lagoon. Allow 40 minutes to drive it, dodging potholes. The road runs through Faralya to dead-end at Kabak. \"Dolmu\u015fes\" ply the sealed road, but stop 1 km out where it becomes a dirt lane descending into the valley. 2WD vehicles may continue, but with a trailer / caravan you might need 4WD to get out. \n is the village car park, where some guy will demand a parking fee that he's just thought of, pocket it and not issue a receipt. In 2024 the going rate was 200 TL. The dirt lane continues another 500 m to the beach, form your own judgement on whether your vehicle can safely continue. \nSee below for the Lycian Way hiking trail.\nGet around.\nWalk everywhere, the dirt lanes are ruinous to cars.\n Many places will allow you to pitch your own tent. \nConnect.\nAs of Aug 2024, Kabak and the road from \u00d6l\u00fcdeniz have 4G from Turkcell and T\u00fcrk Telekom, but the signal from Vodafone gives out south of Faralya. 5G has not rolled out in Turkey."}
{"id": "16585", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16585", "title": "Kabale", "text": "Kabale is one of the major cities in South-Western Uganda with major tourist attractions such as Lake Bunyonyi and the Bwindi Impenetrable Forest.\nUnderstand.\nKabale is about 420 km (260\u00a0mi), by road, southwest of Kampala, Uganda's capital and largest city and lies 2,000 m (6,600\u00a0ft) above sea level. \nKabale is a market town and a transport hub as the main road from Kampala continues through the town, to the border with Rwanda at Katuna, 10 km (6.2\u00a0mi) south of Kabale. Another road continues westwards to Kisoro, where travellers may continue to either the Democratic Republic of the Congo or the Rwandan town of Ruhengeri. Kabale is often used as a stop-off point for people visiting Lake Bunyonyi but is a far less convenient base than Kisoro for those who want to visit the two parks famous for mountain gorilla tracking: Mgahinga National Park and Bwindi Impenetrable National Park. The Virunga Mountains along the Rwandan border are visible from the town.\nGetting in.\nGateway, Bismarkan, Abapakasi, and Jaguarhave buses from Kampala take around 7 hours. This usually costs Ush 40,000. Also, buses that go to Kigali, Rwanda like Trinity and Volcano usually have a stopover in Kabale town and are a good way to travel to Kabale from Kampala. These may go up to Ush 60,000. \nAlternatively, one can connect from Mbarara City to Kabale. Buses from Kampala to Mbarara take around 4 hours, these include Global and Tausi majorly. From Kabale, commuter taxis, usually charge around Ush 20,000 to 25,000 or less to Kabale and this takes about 2.5 to 3 hours to reach Kabale."}
{"id": "16586", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16586", "title": "Kabankalan", "text": "Kabankalan is a city in the province of Negros Occidental, Philippines.\nUnderstand.\nAccording to the 2015 census, it has a population of 182,000.\nSleep.\nZaycoland Resort and Hotel, has 16 luxurious guest rooms including family, bridal, and master suites, and amenities such as an Olympic sized swimming pool, kiddie pool, volleyball court, children's play area, and a restaurant. Tel. +63 34 4712 880, 7467 055 Mobile. +63 949 6262727. A.C. room with own bathroom and cable TV from."}
{"id": "16587", "revid": "1822", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16587", "title": "Kabardino-Balkaria", "text": "Kabardino-Balkaria is a region in the Russian Caucasus bordering Karachay-Cherkessia to the west, Stavropol Krai to the north, North Ossetia to the east, and Georgia.\nUnderstand.\nKabardino-Balkaria, a republic named after its native Kabardin and Balkar people, is one of Russia's poorest regions. Two ethnic territories form Kabardino-Balkaria: one predominantly of Kabardin (who speak a Caucasian language) and the other predominantly Balkar (who speak a Turkic language). There is also a significant Russian population. The Kabardians and the Balkars are Sunni Muslims. In 1944, Stalin accused the Balkars of collaborating with Nazi Germany and deported the entire population, removing their name from the republic's title. They were allowed to return only in 1957.\nIn 1992, the region was born. The republic felt pray to the instability afflicting other parts of the region and to the contagion of conflict in nearby Chechnya. In 2005, militants attacked security forces in the capital; many people were killed in the incident.\nAlmost all of the population live on agriculture, the main industrial backbone for the Kabardian economy. The tourism sector is rapidly growing, due to proper capital investment, but has attracted few tourists so far.\nTalk.\nThe Kabardin speak Kabardian, a northwest Caucasian language of which \"Cherkess\" is a dialect; the Balkar speak Balkar, a Turkic language virtually identical to \"Karachay.\" Fortunately for the visitor, all are fluent in Russian. No English is understood, and this is not likely to change for a good while.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nVirtually all enter via Nalchik, which is easily reached from the airport at Mineralnye Vody in Stavropol Krai.\nBy train.\nTrains from Moscow leave every day.\nDo.\nMountain climbing.\nMountain climbing is the biggest attraction of Kabardino-Balkaria, and here it's a serious sport. The most popular climb is undoubtedly Mount Elbrus, as it is one of the Seven Summits, and is a fairly easy climb in technical terms (Russian Grade: 2B). But bear in mind it's one of the world's deadliest climbs in terms of fatalities per climber\u2014it's a long climb to the top and the mountain often has dangerous and unpredictable weather. Dykhtau and Koshtan-Tau are considerably more technically challenging climbs. Two of Georgia's highest peaks, Shkhara and Ushba, are also climbable from the Russian side. Although these climbs are illegal, since you cross the Russian-Georgian border, border enforcement is lax at 17,000 ft. The Shkhara climb is a lot easier and safer on the southern face.\nBecause dealing with Russian officialdom is hellish bordering on impossible, it's best to embark on a mountaineering expedition via guided tour (the tour agencies pick you up from Mineralnye Vody or Nalchik right at the airport and act as your intermediary with all Russian officials. Russia-based tour agencies are much cheaper than Western ones:\nLearn.\nKabardino-Balkarian State University\nStay safe.\nWhile not as involved in the general conflict across the North Caucasus, Kabardino-Balkaria, especially Nalchik, has been attacked repeatedly by rebel and terrorist forces. Expect very tight security, realize that this is an unstable and dangerous part of the world, and make your travel plans accordingly.\nThe North Caucasus is an unstable and potentially dangerous region. Short-term travel restrictions are sometimes applied in relation to ongoing security operations with little or no notice. The border crossings to Georgia and Azerbaijan are subject to frequent, sometimes lengthy closures.\nConnect.\nMobile.\nIn Kabardino-Balkaria there are three federal GSM operators (MTS, Beeline, Megafon) and they often have offers that give you a SIM card for free or at least very cheap. If you are planning to stay a while and to keep in touch with Kabardino-Balkarian or other North-Caucasus people, then you should consider buying a local SIM card instead of going on roaming. If you buy a SIM card from a shop you'll need your passport for identification. It only takes five minutes to do the paperwork and it will cost less than $10."}
{"id": "16589", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16589", "title": "Kabarnet", "text": "Kabarnet is a town in the Northern Rift Valley region of Kenya, close to Lake Baringo.\nGet in.\nYou can get in by public transport. Get a bus from Nairobi to Nakuru and then board a matatu to Kabarnet. However, public transport to and around Kabarnet is infrequent and it is advisable to hire or bring your own car. Driving from Nakuru, take the B4 north-bound towards Marigat. About 95 km from Nakuru you will get a right turn off towards Kabarnet. Drive on through the scenic Tugen Hills for about 38 km and you will get to Kabarnet town. Both the B4 and the road leading to Kabarnet (C105) are in good condition and have appropriate road signs.\nYou can also drive from Nakuru to Nabarnet through Eldama Ravine. You will take Saos-Tenges-Kabarnet Road (all paved)\nAccording to the Kenyan Tourism Board, there is an airstrip near the town.\nGet around.\nAs mentioned, most roads are in good condition. However, public transport is scarce and infrequent. Best means of getting around is to hire a car or bring your own.\nDrink.\nKabarnet Hotel offers the best view of Kerio Valley as you take your drink. Here you can get Kenyan beer (Tusker) and imported wines and spirits."}
{"id": "16592", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16592", "title": "Kaberneeme", "text": "Kaberneeme is a small village on the northern coast of Estonia. It's 40 km east of Tallinn on Kaberneeme peninsula.\nUnderstand.\nKaberneeme is a village in J\u00f5el\u00e4htme Parish, Harju County in northern Estonia. It is known for its beach. Kaberneeme. The area has traditionally been connected with fishing and maritime activities. Today, tourism and recreation are important to the village, particularly during the warmer months. The surrounding forests and coastline provide opportunities for walking, cycling and enjoying nature. Kaberneeme is also close to several other coastal villages and natural areas in northern Estonia.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nBus schedules are easily available online \u2013 read more under Estonia#Get around."}
{"id": "16595", "revid": "132185", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16595", "title": "Kabli", "text": "Kabli is a small quiet coastal village in southwestern Estonia.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe village is located next to the Tallinn-Riga highway around 10 km from the Latvian border.\nBy bus.\nBus schedules are easily available online \u2013 read more under Estonia#Get around.\nDo.\nThe beach is the biggest attraction of the village.\nKabli is also a place where the birds migrate in May and October every year - they can be observed from the bird watchtower."}
{"id": "16598", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16598", "title": "Kabul", "text": "Kabul is the capital and largest city of Afghanistan. A crowded metropolis pressed into a basin of the Hindu Kush mountains, its bazaars, battle-scarred buildings and traffic-choked streets bear the traces of an ancient trading city, rapid urban expansion, periods of cosmopolitan ambition, and nearly half a century of war. Since the Taliban returned to power in 2021, the front lines have receded and security has improved, but poverty, choking pollution and the oppressive government \u2013 one that has forced women and girls from much of public life \u2013 cast a heavy pall over the city. Yet Kabul is more than the rule imposed upon it. Millions of Kabulis carry on with lives shaped \u2013 but not wholly defined \u2013 by upheaval and repression.\nIs Kabul a must-visit city? No. Can a visit be disentangled entirely from the Taliban government? Also no. But Kabul is not the Taliban, and travellers who come with open eyes will encounter a city and a people far more complicated than the regime that rules them. Even setting the Taliban aside, Kabul is a rough and hardscrabble city, best approached by experienced travellers or those travelling with government support.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nWhile the origin of Kabul is unknown, archaeological evidence shows settlement around the 6th century BCE and substantial defensive works dating from at least the 2nd century BCE. From the 1st century CE to the 8th century CE, Kabul and the surrounding basin formed an important center of Buddhism, although for much of this period Kabul was overshadowed by Kapisa, the principal settlement in the Kabul River basin until approximately the mid-7th century.\nThroughout its early history, the Kabul region was controlled by numerous political powers, including the Achaemenid Persians, Alexander the Great and his successors, the Seleucid Empire, the Mauryan Empire, the Greco-Bactrian and Indo-Greek kingdoms, the Kushans, the Sasanians, and the Hephthalites. After about 660 CE, Kabul became the seat of the Turk Shahis and subsequently the Hindu Shahis, who resisted Muslim expansion.\nAround 870, Kabul was finally conquered by the Muslim Saffarids, although permanent Muslim control was established only later under the Ghaznavids. The city subsequently passed through the hands of the Samanids, Ghaznavids, Ghurids and Khwarazmshahs before being destroyed by Mongol armies around 1220. It was later ruled by the Chagatai khans, Timurids, Mughals, Afsharids, Durranis and Barakzais. The famous Moroccan traveller Ibn Battuta passed through Kabul in 1333, describing it as \"formerly a vast town\" that had been reduced to a village inhabited by people his account called \"Afghans\".\nUnder Tamerlane and his successors in the 14th and 15th centuries, Kabul gradually recovered from its destruction by the Mongols and developed into a regional center of trade. In 1504, the city was captured by the Timurid prince Babur, who later founded the Mughal Empire.\nIn 1747, Kabul came under control of the Durrani, or Afghan, Empire. In 1775, Kabul became the empire's capital, although the empire later fell into dynastic and tribal conflict. In 1839, British and Indian forces occupied Kabul and established a British-backed government there. They were very unpopular among the local population, who revolted in 1841. In January 1842, most of the roughly 16,000 British and Indian civilians and soldiers attempting to flee from Kabul to Jalalabad were killed or captured, a famous blunder known as the Massacre of Elphinstone's Army. The first Flashman novel is a fictionalized account of this. The British returned in 1878 during the 2nd Anglo-Afghan War and occupied Kabul in 1879; although they suffered serious losses, they ultimately achieved their political aims and withdrew.\nIn the early 20th century, the city's electricity supply was expanded and Darul Aman Palace was constructed as part of a planned government district. The 1930s to 1960s were a period of growth and modernization in Kabul. Kabul University was opened; roads were paved; modern shops, offices, and schools were opened; shopping centers and a cinema were opened; and Kabul Zoo opened. The city also saw a vibrant tourism industry appear, helped by the Istanbul\u2013New Delhi \"Hippie Trail\", which passed through Kabul in the 1960s and 1970s.\nThe 1970s and 1980s brought a turn for the worse. The city saw two coups, in 1973 and 1978. The second coup was carried out by the Marxist PDPA and was followed a year later by a Soviet military intervention to support the PDPA government. From 1979 to 1989, the Soviet Union maintained its military headquarters in Kabul and supported the Afghan government. After the Soviets left, the government collapsed in 1992 and left rival factions to fight over the city, leaving tens of thousands dead and the city devastated. By 1994, much of the city was without electricity or water. In 1996, the political movement known as the Taliban captured the city, killing the former (pre-1992) president and imposing notoriously strict Islamic rule in the areas it controlled.\nA US-led military force invaded Afghanistan in October 2001, bombing strategic installations throughout the city to rout the governing Taliban, who quickly fled the city. The city became the capital of the Afghan Transitional Authority and later of the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan. It saw many suicide bombings after 2001, with particularly deadly attacks occurring during the late 2010s. In late 2008, responsibility for the city's security was passed from the NATO-led ISAF to the Afghan National Police and the Afghan National Army. After 2001, billions of dollars in aid and foreign investment were used to improve the city. Most of the major roads were paved and improved, government buildings were extensively renovated, new hotels opened, the zoo was renovated, many museums reopened, and utilities were extensively reconstructed. Some modern-style tower blocks and a handful of glitzy shopping malls also appeared, while electricity and water supplies improved, although neither became entirely reliable.\nIn 2021, the Taliban recaptured the city after a campaign of about four months, overthrowing the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan and reestablishing the Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan. This marked a significant shift, with the Taliban imposing notoriously strict Islamic governance leading to reduced foreign presence and restricted women's rights.\nSince the Taliban's takeover, many development projects have been halted and internationally funded reconstruction efforts have slowed significantly, with limited progress on some major infrastructure projects. Economic constraints and Taliban restrictions have also sharply reduced private construction, although municipal road-building and other public works have since accelerated. That said, roads and other infrastructure in outlying areas remain in poor condition. Electricity and water supplies remain highly inconsistent, with electricity outages occurring several times daily as of 2026 and water supply being nearly as unreliable. On the positive side, the security situation in the capital has improved significantly, and as of 2026, the city is largely safe for foreign visitors \u2013 foreign women included \u2013 in strong contrast to the plight of Afghan women under the Taliban's rule.\nClimate.\nKabul's climate is greatly influenced by its location in a valley at 1,800\u00a0m (5,900\u00a0ft). Summers (Jun-Sep) are hot and dry, averaging from the high 20s to the mid-30s \u00b0C (80-95\u00a0\u00b0F) with next to no precipitation. Autumn (Oct-Nov) is temperate and sees very little precipitation. Winters (Dec-Mar) are cold and the time of year which sees the most precipitation (mostly snow, but also ice, freezing rain, and sleet on warmer days). January is the coldest month, averaging 4/-7\u00a0\u00b0C (39/19\u00a0\u00b0F). Spring (late Mar-early June) is temperate with rain tapering off by early May.\nThe city lies in a valley and some villages on the edge of the city are a few hundred meters higher and thus cooler in the summer and colder and snowier in the winters. In winter, heavy snowfall can disrupt roads leading to and from Kabul, most notoriously the highway north through the Salang Pass, which is periodically closed due to snow and avalanches.\nOrientation.\nCentral Kabul straddles the Kabul River. The Old City, including Mandawi Bazaar, lies south of the river, while Shahr-e Naw (meaning New City) to the north is the principal traveller-oriented district, largely walkable with hotels and restaurants but also, sadly, numerous street children (see Stay safe). Wazir Akbar Khan, just east of Shahr-e Naw, is one of Kabul's wealthiest districts and another center for accommodation and dining, particularly at the higher end. Beyond the central city, Kabul sprawls into numerous outer districts. Hills funnel traffic onto a limited number of routes, so places that look close on a map can be slow to reach.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nArrival.\nThe trip from the airport to the city center via taxi should cost about AFN 300-500 (2026). During daytime, you can get a taxi for cheaper (AFN 100\u2013200) if you exit the airport and hail a taxi on the road outside the main gate. See Taxi scams for tips on dealing with Kabuli taxi drivers.\nDeparture.\nWhen flying out you will probably end up in Car Park C, and will have to get the shuttle bus to the terminal building. Or you may even be dropped off at the airport gate and have to walk all the way in. When flying out expect multiple ticket, passport and baggage checks, so allow plenty of time before your flight. As of 2026, you are allowed to leave the country by air with at most US$5000 cash of any currency (as opposed to only US$500 when exiting by land).\nInternational destinations.\nInternational destinations include:\nDomestic destinations.\nWhile the airport is not bad for a developing country, expect very basic conditions at other Afghan airports.\nBy bus.\nPrivate bus operators serve some destinations in full-size coaches. Coaches from reputable companies are reasonably modern and comfortable, although rather slow compared to shared taxis.\nBy minibus or shared taxi.\nMinibuses and shared taxis arrive and depart from six official locations around the city. Shared taxis and most minibuses leave when full rather than on fixed schedules. Shared taxis have no particular appearance of their own \u2013\u00a0they look the same as city taxis (i.e. turquoise).\nYou may be asked if you want a \"special taxi\". This means you pay for the entire taxi instead of sharing it with strangers. If you'd prefer this, try to discern the going per-seat rate for shared taxis to your destination, multiply it by the number of seats, and then negotiate a discount. Drivers are usually happy to give a discount if it means they can depart sooner instead of having to wait for additional passengers. You can usually also buy extra seats (or an entire backseat) to give yourself extra room in a shared taxi.\nGet around.\nTravel permits.\nAs of 2026, travel to any province in Afghanistan (including Kabul) requires a permit. In Kabul, these can be obtained at the on Shash Darak Road for AFN 1000 each.\nBy bus.\nThe Millie Bus (Afghanistan's government-run bus service) operates routes in Kabul, but services are unreliable, and many buses are old. Taxis are the far easier option for getting around the city.\nBy taxi.\nAs of 2026, taxis cost AFN 100\u2013200 within the city, depending on destination and negotiation. Destinations farther afield, such as the airport or Qargha Lake, will cost AFN 200\u2013400. If you don't negotiate hard, or if you take the first taxi you find, expect to pay about double these prices as a foreigner. Some drivers speak basic English, but they may charge more and may be hard to find other than near major hotels or the airport. After dark, taxis are scarce, so keep trusted taxi contacts as a backup.\nIn 2023, the Taliban-run traffic authority re-registered all taxi drivers in Afghanistan to reduce kidnappings and crimes involving unlicensed taxis. (As part of this change, all licensed taxis in the country were repainted from yellow to turquoise.) The initiative was deemed a success: Kabul police claimed that kidnappings had fallen \"nearly to zero\". Nowadays, being overcharged is your biggest threat. See Taxi scams below for tips on how to avoid being overcharged by taxi drivers in Kabul.\nAlthough there are no legal restrictions on foreigners as of 2026, \"taxi drivers may refuse to pick up women\" if they are not wearing a veil or a face covering, or if they are travelling without a male companion, as these rules apply to Afghan women. Aside from the veil, however, these rules are regularly ignored. Afghan women must sit in the back seat.\nThere are no ride-hailing services in Kabul as of 2026.\nBy car.\nCar rentals are limited, and driving yourself is discouraged due to heavy traffic and poor road conditions. The frequent checkpoints in the city can be an annoyance. Afghanistan drives on the right side, although right-hand-drive cars are almost as common as left-hand-drive cars.\nBy foot.\nCentral Kabul is compact, making walking viable in spring and fall. Summers are hot and dusty, while winters bring snow and mud. Pavements are scarce, and crossing roads requires caution.\nSee.\nDespite its long history, Kabul is rather short on historical sights. Centuries of warfare and decades of urban sprawl have taken their toll. Most contemporary attractions are disappointingly lackluster, poorly maintained, and surprisingly expensive. The city is more compelling around Shah-Do Shamshira Mosque and the Mandawi Bazaar, offering a glimpse of old Kabul amid a maze of crowded lanes and bustling markets.\nMarkets.\nThe Mandawi Bazaar (see Buy below) has a couple of unique markets tucked away amongst the chaos. You (hopefully) won't be buying anything here, but they might be of interest from a dark tourism perspective.\nBuy.\nMoney.\nYou can change currency (e.g. USD to AFN) in banks or at local money changers on the street. If you do it at local money changers, there is no fee, but make sure you know the exchange rate before attempting. A popular place to change currency is at the tables on the street in front of Majid Mall \u2013 rates there are generally fine.\nATMs and banking services are gradually expanding. \nEat.\nFood is a definite bright spot of Kabul, as it is cheap, delicious, and safe \u2013 except for street food, which is only cheap and delicious. Best not to risk the latter unless you've lived in Afghanistan a while. Restaurants open and close with surprising frequency, so it is a good idea to check whether a place is still operating before heading out. Most restaurant menus are in Dari; take a photo of the menu and ask an AI chatbot to explain it to you.\nDrink.\nTea.\nAfghanistan is a tea country, and Afghans drink enormous amounts of it, especially green tea (\"chay-e sabz\") and black tea (\"chay-e siyah\"). Traditional \"chaikhanas\" are the analogue to caf\u00e9s in the Arab world. However, much of Kabul\u2019s traditional urban fabric was badly damaged during decades of war, and the city\u2019s subsequent rapid and chaotic expansion contributed to the decline of its traditional tea-house culture. Today, Kabul lacks a conspicuous \"chaikhana\" scene, although traditional tea houses can still be found around the Mandawi Bazaar and elsewhere in the old commercial center.\nBear in mind that \"chaikhanas\" have traditionally been overwhelmingly male spaces, and this convention has been reinforced by the Taliban\u2019s institutionalized misogyny, so female travellers may be ushered to a separate room, simply raise eyebrows, or occasionally be refused admission altogether.\nCoffee.\nCoffee proliferated during the Islamic Republic of 2004-2021, catering largely to young urbanites and expats, but the scene has contracted considerably since the Taliban takeover.\nAlcohol.\nIt ain't happening. Under the Taliban, Kabul is completely dry.\nSleep.\nKabul has a range of hotels and guesthouses. Guesthouses, often large Pakistani-style mansions, are common in Wazir Akbar Khan and Shar-e Naw, and may offer better value than hotels. Most rates are negotiable, particularly at the budget end \u2013 try booking through WhatsApp instead of online booking platforms.\nAs of 2026, city electricity is intermittent and city water may be off or intermittent for days at a time. If these utilities are essential for you, it is worth asking your accommodation if they have contingency systems (generators, water tanks, etc.).\nConnect.\nMobile phones.\nThe mobile telephone system in Kabul is well developed, reliable, and provides a decent signal throughout most of the city. 5G has not arrived as of 2026 but 4G is widely available. Roshan is considered the best mobile provider within Kabul. If you're venturing outside of Kabul, either Roshan or Afghan wireless should work. Many Afghans use both with dual SIM card slots, switching between providers as needed.\nYou can buy a SIM card upon arrival at the airport. Outside the airport, both Roshan and Afghan wireless have sales offices within the city. Bring your passport and be prepared to sit a while.\nInternet.\nWired internet and WiFi in Kabul is not good. Expect slow speeds and intermittent outages. If you require connectivity, ensure you can hotspot via your mobile phone and that your mobile plan provides a sufficiently large amount of data, or consider satellite internet.\nStay safe.\nCrime.\nThe days when one couldn't venture out after sunset in Kabul are over, and safety has significantly improved since the Taliban takeover in 2021. Remain cautious, however. Kabul is a poverty-stricken city, and if you are recognizable as a foreigner you will stand out. Don't walk alone outside Shahr-e Naw (the New City) after dark. Avoid flashing large amounts of cash at any time of day.\nIt\u2019s common to encounter children begging for money, particularly in Shar-e Naw. While their requests may seem harmless, giving money can attract more children, quickly escalating into a crowd that\u2019s difficult to manage. This can pose a safety risk, as it draws attention and may invite pickpocketing or other opportunistic crimes. To stay safe, politely decline and avoid engaging further.\nIn traditional residential areas near the city wall, conservative locals may view outsiders with suspicion, and children may throw stones or set dogs on strangers. Don't go over the mountains near the city wall \u2013 no one lives there and, according to locals, you can be robbed or shot at.\nScams.\nDue to decades of foreign occupation, Kabulis are used to dealing with foreigners and adept at taking their money. While outright robbery is rare since the Taliban takeover, overcharging and short-change scams are common. Always know how much you should pay and always count your change when it is handed back to you. Hide your camera when entering tourist attractions to avoid being hit with a surprise camera fee. If you can, ask for an itemized receipt for anything you pay for.\nTaxi scams.\nMany, if not most taxi drivers attempt to overcharge foreigners. Always negotiate a price before you ride. If possible, ask a local the appropriate price to your destination. Don't negotiate numbers verbally; instead, type the prices on your phone (e.g. a calculator app) so you and the driver agree on the same figure. Ensure the driver is aware that the price is for the taxi, not per person, as many will ridiculously claim at the end of your ride. When your ride is over, most will ask for more money for whatever reason (petrol prices being a popular one in 2026); show them the price you pre-negotiated on your phone, pay without flashing excess cash, and quickly be on your way.\nTaliban.\nTaliban foot soldiers mostly ignore tourists, and, unlike Afghan women, foreign women face no special restrictions or maltreatment. However, if you are not discernibly foreign and not abiding by Taliban dress codes (veil and face covering for women, beard length for men, etc.), then you are more likely to attract attention. They may check your documents \u2013 be sure to carry your passport and travel permits on you at all times. Usually, if they acknowledge you at all, they'll just want to chat. That said, do not take photos or video of sensitive locations, such as government buildings, military bases and embassies, nor of government vehicles or military equipment (commandeered American Humvees included). The Taliban will not take kindly to this.\nStay healthy.\nFood safety.\nRestaurant food in Kabul is generally safe, and Afghans are excellent cooks. However, you should use the old traveller's trick: find restaurants that are popular with locals (locals tend not to return to restaurants with poor food hygiene). In Kabul, it is possible to use Google Maps to find restaurants \u2013 look for those with a reasonably high number of (positive) reviews. Remember, it is more the number of reviews that counts, and less the average rating. \nThat said, there are foods you should avoid, even in restaurants, namely those that are uncooked, unpasteurized, or may have been prepared with contaminated water. Stay well away from dairy products such as ice cream. Raw salads, unpeeled fruit and fresh juices are a risk. Ask for bottled water if you can, and avoid water served to you in a glass (hot tea is fine). Essentially, stick to food that is hot.\nStreet food is another matter altogether: don't do it. And don't say we didn't warn you.\nPollution.\nKabul is one of the most polluted cities in the world. Air pollution is a serious problem, particularly in winter. Vehicle exhaust is a major contributor year-round. Winter brings smoke from household heating. In summer, heating smoke is replaced by dust. This is all trapped within Kabul's mountainous geography. It might be worth bringing a particulate mask (e.g. an N95) on your visit, especially if travelling in winter, as they are difficult to find in the city. Travellers with asthma should carry their inhalers.\nGround and water pollution is just as serious. Sewage infrastructure is limited. Garbage accumulates almost everywhere. The pitiable Kabul River is essentially an open sewer and trash receptacle. The open drains that run alongside the roadways are heavily contaminated. Drink bottled water, and bottled water only. Wash your hands frequently, especially before eating. Avoid stagnant water and open drains like the plague. Clean and disinfect cuts promptly, as these can quickly become infected.\nHealthcare.\nThe healthcare system in Kabul is limited, especially since the Taliban takeover. For severe illness or trauma, most embassies recommend medical evacuation (e.g. to Islamabad or Dubai).\nCope.\nEmbassies.\nThe operations of many embassies were suspended when the Taliban took power in 2021. The embassies listed below are open and provide consular services as of 2026.\nGo next.\nTravelling within Afghanistan outside of Kabul (and within Kabul, technically), as of 2026, will require a permit. See Travel permits above."}
{"id": "16599", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16599", "title": "Kabwe", "text": "Kabwe is a city in Central Zambia. It is the capital of Central Province.\nUnderstand.\nWhen Zambia was the colony of Northern Rhodesia, Kabwe's British name was Broken Hill, due to the hill just south of town which had a natural shaft going through it. This became the location of a large lead and zinc mine, which in turn became the major employer of the town. The mine was later closed, and now has only been partially reopened. Because of the amount of lead in the surrounding soil and local water, Kabwe today is one of the 5 most polluted places on earth.\nDuring the mid-1900s Kabwe played an important role in Zambia's history, as it was here that the movement for independence from Britain really gained momentum and finally reached critical mass. Since then, the national and local government agencies have invested little in the city, and its infrastructure has deteriorated badly (in stark contrast to Lusaka's growth).\nGet in.\nThere is good luxury bus service going north from Lusaka and going south from the Copperbelt cities. One of the best companies running buses is Mazhandu - but be prepared to listen to (or try to ignore?) a loud preacher quoting from the Bible during the first half-hour or so on the rides leaving Lusaka. The \"bus station\", for all companies, is on the street in front of Big Bite.\nGet around.\nIn its heyday many of Kabwe's streets were paved and lighted. Today the light posts are still there but except for right in town the lights are all gone, and all but the main streets have deteriorated back into dirt roads with serious bumps and holes, so go slowly and carefully if you drive a car. Many locals use bicycles and most simply walk, and there are a lot of dirt-path \"shortcuts\" between the named streets, particularly going across the railway tracks between the suburb of Luangwa and the main part of town. The blue-and-white minibuses are to be found all over town and are a good value, and the driver will stop to pick you up wherever you stand, but be prepared to have to wait to get off only where he wants to stop. If you are driving, put lots of space between yourself and these guys, because they drive dangerously - all the locals will tell you this. In Zambia, as in most former British colonies, traffic on a 2-way street drives on the left side of the road.\nSee.\nThere is generally really not much for the tourist to see in Kabwe, but it is so laid-back that it is a good way to see what life is like in a regular Zambian town, yet large enough that a tourist would not really feel out-of-place.\nDo.\nOne of the best things to do in Kabwe is to go hiking at the actual Broken Hill, just east of the railway tracks at the far south end of town. Hike up the hills to the lakes: these were the mine shafts, and are now very deep lakes which provide solitude in a beautiful setting.\nBuy.\nAs Kabwe is not really a tourist destination, there's nothing particular to be bought here. But of course there is a typical African market, south of Independence Avenue just west of the railway tracks, and you can get great fresh food at good prices. Be sure to use your negotiating skills - it's the normal way of doing business in Zambia outside of the town stores. Expect the seller's first price to be approximately double what he/she is willing to accept, and accordingly make your first counteroffer far below the acceptance price. If you can engage the seller in a bit of friendly arguing, better still.\nGo next.\nSeveral companies, notably Powertools, run buses south to Lusaka and north to the Copperbelt. The \"bus station\" is on the street in front of Big Bite on the south side of Independence Avenue.\nIf you are driving, Independence Avenue becomes Great North Road outside of Kabwe, leading north to the Copperbelt cities and the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and leading south to Lusaka, Livingstone, and Zimbabwe. It is paved its entire length inside Zambia (but in mid-2010 the area between Zimba and Livingstone was being repaved and several places have a detour onto a parallel dirt road)."}
{"id": "16602", "revid": "2256205", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16602", "title": "Ka\u00e7anik", "text": " Ka\u00e7anik/Ka\u010danik, or Ka\u00e7aniku, in Kosovo, is a city of peace and quiet, its houses with pointed red roofs being scattered along the verdant mountain slopes.\nKa\u00e7anik is the first major Kosovar town en route when coming from North Macedonia, except for the border village of Hani i Elezit/Elez Han.\nThe town of Ka\u00e7anik has 15,634 inhabitants, while the municipality has 33,409 inhabitants\nGet in.\nThe best way to get to Ka\u00e7anik is by bus from Pristina. \nYou can also catch a bus from Skopje. Most bus companies that run from Istanbul to Pristina continue onward and terminate in Ka\u00e7anik. There are several direct buses from Belgrade (7 hours, 1 day bus &amp; 2 night buses, run by Kosovo Albanian companies, cost about \u20ac10, stops depending on the route in Ni\u0161 or Kru\u0161evac, Pristina and other towns on the road, information dates from February 2005). \nAdditionally, on a very questionable road through the mountains you can arrive from Tirana.\nYou can reach Ka\u00e7anik from Pristina/ Ferizaj and Hani I Elezit by train. The train runs two times a day to both directions. \nTaxis are driving you for \u20ac5 from Ferizaj to Ka\u00e7anik. You meet the drivers at the bus stop/train station in Ferizaj. Taxi from Skopje is around \u20ac10. \nGet around.\nFrom the bus station everything is within walking distance.\nThe community of Ka\u00e7anik (ka\u00e7anikas, kachanikas) is very kind and pleasant, so don't miss it, visit this beauty and peace city!\nSee.\nVisit the mosque \"Koxha Sinan Pasha\". It is one of the oldest buildings in the town. The mosque is near the bus station and bridge (south). Take a walk at the river.\nDo.\nDon't miss a walk over the promenade in the evening! All citizens of the town, doesn't matter if young or old, are strolling there in the evenings. Talk a walk - you won't forget it! \nYou can take a trip with the car to \"Ivaj\u00eb\" and \"Shtraza\". The landscape around the two small villages is beautiful. \nAsk in a local bar/caffe for hiking tours. Many locals go for hiking every Sunday. The Sunday hiking tours are a tradition in Ka\u00e7anik.\nEat.\nIf you are passionate about traditional good food, it is worth to visit \"Guri i Zi\" restaurant, located in south east of Kacanik. The forest location and the mountain view gives you a pleasant relaxing space where you can enjoy time and good food. They also offer renting small cabins for relatively cheap prices. Phone number is +383 46 181 200.\nOne of the best fish restaurants around Ka\u00e7anik, restaurant \"Trofta\" is located in Ivaja village.The fish are swimming next to your table in small pools and are caught when you order your meal. They serve the fish with salad and traditional bread.\nRestaurant Fontana is a family place who celebrates weeding and another parties. Location: Kaqanik, Magjistrale. Phone Number: +383 45 641 740.\n\"Te Bunari\" is a great place to eat and drink. It is located right at the entrance of the city and the trees around it are amazing for a summer breeze.\nDrink.\nOne of the best places to go to enjoy a drink are: \nGo next.\nSouth: Skopje in North Macedonia. You can go there by bus or taxi. \nNorth: Pristina (capital) or Ferizaj. It is a nice trip by train to Ferizaj (ticket approx. \u20ac1.50).\nWest: Brezovica. It is a small village in the mountains. In winter you can go skiing."}
{"id": "16603", "revid": "1342202", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16603", "title": "Ka\u015f", "text": "Ka\u015f (pronounced \"Kaash\") is a beach resort in Lycia on the Mediterranean coast of Turkey. It attracts a mixed crowd of British, German and Russian tourists but isn't on the package-holiday circuit, as its beaches are small and stony, and there are better beaches within a shorter transfer from the airports. In 2022 its population was 9084. \nUnderstand.\nKa\u015f has beach activities, and ancient ruins in its hills, but is mostly a relaxing sort of place. Standing at 36\u00b012\u2032 N, on about the same latitude as Tunis in North Africa, it has a subtropical Mediterranean climate. Summers are hot and sticky, with average daytime highs of 35\u00b0C, with very little rain. Winters are mild with daytime temperatures around 15\u00b0C, albeit with occasionally very heavy rains. September is a good time to visit, with warm seas but without the sweltering summer heat.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nDalaman Airport () is the closest, with seasonal package flights from Europe and the UK. It's 180 km west of Kalkan, and the drive might take 2\u00bd hours via D400 (toll tunnel). You could take the airport bus as far as Fethiye then change to the regular bus (below) or taxi.\nBy bus.\nThe closest major city is Antalya, where you can take a bus from the bus terminal (Otogar\u0131) which runs every 45 minutes and the journey usually takes about 4 hours. Look for buses with signs \"Ka\u015f\" or \"Kalkan\" on the windshield.\nBuses from Istanbul run 2 or 3 times a day and take 15 hr 30 min via Gebze, Bursa, Izmir, Ayd\u0131n, Mu\u011fla, Akyaka, Dalaman, Fethiye and Kalkan. Bus lines include Pamukkale and Flixbus.\n is the new bus station, 5 km east on the highway to Demre. In other Turkish out-of-town bus stations, a \"dolmu\u015f\" ride downtown is included in your inter-city ticket. That appears not to apply here, but try asking when booking. The dolmu\u015f will bring you to the old \"otogar\" in town centre (main square off Atat\u00fcrk Bvd) but for accommodation on the peninsula you might as well take a taxi. Local buses along the coast (from as far away as Fethiye) may also bring you to the old otogar.\nBy boat.\nMeis Express and Meis Ferry Lines sail daily to the Greek island of Kastellorizo (\"Meis\" in Turkish), taking 20 min. A day-trip from Ka\u015f gives you six hours in Kastellorizo, but the reverse day-trip is not possible. Ka\u015f ferry pier is in the marina south end of town.\nGet around.\nKa\u015f is small and easy to walk around. Dolmu\u015fes (mini buses) run to the \u00c7ukurba\u011f peninsula, to villages such as \u00c7ukurba\u011f (where Phellos Ancient City is) and along the coast to Kalkan and Demre (which are other small cities, not a part of Ka\u015f).\nTo go to \u00c7ukurba\u011f Peninsula near Ka\u015f, look for bus station right near Merkez S\u00fcleyman Cami mosque in the center of the city near the waterfront, near its northern side there is a bus stop with several mini buses which go to peninsula regularly. As of 2025 the ride costs 50\u20ba.\nMidnight buses to Peninsula are no longer active, and taking a taxi there will, as of 2025, cost 350\u20ba, even when the ride will be just under 10 minutes. Due to the lack of ride-hailing and some kind of agreement between the drivers - they are not willing to negotiate and all name the same price, even starting to be visibly annoyed or angry when someone tries to point that 7 euro for 7 minute long taxi ride is too much for Turkey.\nEat.\nThere are plenty of places in Old Town with good ambiance. Their value for money and cost was good before, but is being gradually changed by hikes in prices in last several years due to economic problem in Turkey, so overall cost of going out moved closer to being on the level of many European cities.\n Town centre has several \"pansiyons\", but the main accommodation strip is the beachfront south along H\u00fck\u00fcmet Cd. \n The campsite is at the foot of the peninsula a short walk from town. Some 4 km further out are the pricier hotels. \nConnect.\nKa\u015f and the coast road have 4G from all Turkish carriers. As of May 2024, 5G has not rolled out in Turkey."}
{"id": "16608", "revid": "2441224", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16608", "title": "Kachemak Bay State Park", "text": "Kachemak Bay State Park is a state park on the shores of Kachemak Bay across from the town of Homer in the state of Alaska.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nThis is Alaska's first and largest park including 400,000 acre of park and wilderness areas.\nLandscape.\nA collection of dramatic bays and steep rocky fjords in a mild maritime climate. A small annual rainfall of 30 inches and average summer temperatures in the 70's (\u00b0F) gave this lovely place the nickname \"summerland\" by the first Russians to arrive on her shores in the 1800s.\nFlora and fauna.\nInhabited by wildlife on land, in the air, and in the ocean, there is so much to see and do here that one could spend a lifetime exploring around. Mountain goats grace the cliffs of remote and beautiful Sadie Cove from the entrance and up to the wilderness lodge of the same name on the south facing shore. Black bears live high in the mountains and can also be seen in the springtime on the shores of the park searching for the first foods of the new season. Bald eagles fly above and in the ocean there are seals, sea lions, humpback whales, orcas, sea otters and sea birds.\nGet in.\nTo get to most areas of the park one needs to hire a water taxi from the town of Homer.\nGet around.\nThe park has a plethora of well maintained hiking trails for the novice as well as the experienced hiker.\nSee.\nThe hiking trails, the glaciers, the Native villages of Seldovia, Port Graham, and Nanwallek, the artist community of Halibut Cove, the dramatically beautiful and remote Sadie Cove, and the head of Kachemak Bay.\nBuy.\nYou\u2019ll probably want to do your shopping in Homer before you depart. Although there are several small villages in or near the park, retail prices are exceptionally high and variety exceptionally small, although Halibut Cove is known for its thriving arts scene and has several galleries.\nEat.\nThe village of Seldovia has a couple of nice places to dine, and Halibut Cove has the famous Saltry restaurant, and Jim Nardelli's floating Espresso stand.\nSleep.\nCamping.\nPermits for camping can be obtained through the Alaska State Parks website. Book early. Yurts are available for nightly rentals through Nomad Shelters in Homer.\nStay safe.\nThis is a wilderness park. Visitor facilities are extremely limited. Bring extra food in case the unpredictable coastal weather delays you. Cell phones may not work in some areas. As always in Alaska, be mindful that you are in bear country and take appropriate precautions, including securing food and water at all times when not in immediate use."}
{"id": "16613", "revid": "2350368", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16613", "title": "Kadavu", "text": "Kadavu is an island in the south of Fiji. Kadavu is in the South Pacific Ocean and is part of the many islands that make up Fiji.\nUnderstand.\nKadavu island is volcanic in origin and rises 3,000 m from the ocean floor.\nSurrounded by hundreds of miles of fringing coral, the island also has 50 miles of the Great Astrolabe barrier reef forming sheltered lagoons and steep outer reef drop-offs.\nIt has few roads, but some cell phones and limited internet access and is a quiet and tranquil traditional island sitting in the trade winds of the original South Pacific.\nKadavu is the fourth largest island of Fiji and is made up of smaller island like Ono an Galoa. Kadavu is 58-km long and 14-km wide and supports a population of 10,000 Fijian people. The main city in Kadavu is Vunisea, which hosts an air strip, hospital, post office and police station. Kadavu's weather is tropical and has an authentic island feel. Some of the natural resources of Kadavu include mountainous jungles and waterfalls, bays fringed by coral reefs and a mangrove forest that provides a habitat to a host of wildlife and birds, including the Kadavu musk parrot. Kadavu's seemingly untouched and natural state makes it a good remote vacation destination.\nKadavu has undergone very little development; it offers limited transportation and there are few if any surface roads that go through the island. The current system of transportation includes a system of boat taxis and ferries. Neither are there any dining out destinations, only some resorts. Most resorts have a relationship with neighboring villages that will allow one to sample traditional Fijian food during village tours, hikes or Meke nights. Kadavu totally integrates the wildlife, the locals and the tourism industry and is one of the leading places in the world for the promotion of environmental conservation and sustainable tourism.\nTalk.\nIn Fiji the islanders speak Fijian, an Austronesian language that was established by the 1997 constitution as the official language of Fiji. Some other languages spoken in Fiji and Kadavu are English and Hindustani. Standard Fijian is based on the language Bau which is associated with eastern Fiji. In Kadavu and other parts of Fiji different dialects have formed differentiating the areas of the island.\nGet in.\nThere are a variety of way to get to and travel in Fiji and Kadavu. The major ways of transportation are to travel by scheduled flight, ferry service, sea plane, yacht transfer or big game boat transfer.\nBy plane.\nSeaplanes allow a more personal experience of Fiji and Kadavu because of the flexibility it offers. Passengers of the seaplanes do not have to wait as long as others for a commercial flight and they are a more private form of travel. This also allows the tourist or guest to sightsee while traveling.\nBy ferry.\nThe Ferry services only offers transportation form island to island in Fiji. Most ferry services are used as water taxis taking guests and visitors from the airports to their desired destination. The ferry service for Kadavu takes guests from Suva to Vunisea or from Vunisea to any other place on the island of Kadavu. These ferries do not only hold guest, live stock freight and goods are being carried aboard the ferries as well. The ferries are not constant and do not run all the time and other forms of transportation may need to be provided beyond the ferries. The Yacht Transfer offers travelers to have a boat experience and explore the waters of Kadavu. This is seen as Kadavu's safari of the sea. Lastly the Game Boat transfer allows for a more interactive experience if the guest likes to fish and have a personal adventure.\nGet around.\nWithin the island most resorts offer guest with Tour Guides or Locals that can takes patrons to the major attractions of the island. Since the road systems are underdeveloped most will experience Kadavu by foot. Trekking excursions offer a one on one experience with nature and a way to experience Fiji like the Fijians. To move from area to are one will other take kayaking trips or use a ferry service. Most travel is done with Long Boats made from fibreglass, using Yamaha motors.\nSee.\nKadavu's rich wild life and natural resources allow for a wide range of activities to partake in on the island. Kadavu is known for being the ultimate diving site. Popular diving sites are the Naigoro Passage which is a massive marine reserve. Also the Manta Reef which inhabits the Manta rays, the Soso Passage where it offers the largest underwater coral garden and lastly the Nacomoto Passage which is home to the eagle rays and cabbage coral. Other activities include:\nDo.\nKadavu is best known for outdoor activities, particularly those connected with the Great Astrolabe Reef, rainforest-covered interior and traditional coastal villages. Most excursions are arranged through resorts because transport between different parts of the island is usually by boat rather than road.\nScuba diving and snorkelling are the principal attractions. The Great Astrolabe Reef runs along Kadavu\u2019s southern coast and contains more than 60 recognized dive sites, including coral gardens, steep drop-offs, passages and manta-ray feeding or cleaning areas. Conditions and currents vary considerably, so dives should be arranged with an experienced local operator. Snorkelling is available from beaches, resort boats and guided excursions to sheltered lagoons and outer-reef sites. \nSea kayaking and paddleboarding provide excellent ways to explore Kadavu\u2019s bays, beaches, mangroves and coastal villages. Guided trips range from short paddles near resorts to multi-day journeys between Kadavu and nearby islands, with accommodation and meals arranged along the route. Local guides are strongly recommended for longer journeys because of changing winds, currents and reef passages.\nHiking and waterfall visits take visitors through rainforest, village plantations and rugged hills to viewpoints, swimming holes and secluded beaches. Trails are often informal paths connecting villages rather than marked recreational routes, so arrange a local guide before attempting a long walk. Some resort excursions combine trekking with birdwatching, village visits and swimming beneath waterfalls. \nKadavu is an important birdwatching destination, with several species found only in the Kadavu group. Forest, mangrove and coastal habitats can be explored on foot with local guides, particularly in the early morning when birds are most active. \nFishing excursions range from reef fishing to offshore sport fishing. Kadavu is particularly known for wahoo, tuna and other pelagic fish along the outer reef. Use a responsible operator, respect locally managed marine areas and confirm whether the catch will be released or retained before departure. \nSome resorts provide access to remote surf breaks along passages through the barrier reef. Many breaks are exposed, shallow and suitable only for intermediate or advanced surfers. Boat transport is normally required, and trips depend on tides, winds and swell conditions. \nA guided village visit offers insight into daily life, local customs and Kadavu\u2019s community-managed conservation areas. Visitors may participate in a \"sevusevu\" welcome ceremony, drink kava, see traditional dancing or crafts, and share a locally prepared meal. Dress modestly, remove hats when requested and ask permission before photographing residents or ceremonies. \nEat.\nThere are few standalone restaurants on Kadavu, particularly away from Vunisea. Most visitors eat at their resort, guesthouse or homestay, and many remote properties sell accommodation on a full-board basis because alternative dining options cannot easily be reached. Confirm which meals are included when booking and notify the property in advance about allergies or dietary requirements. \nMeals commonly make use of fresh fish and other seafood, coconut milk, tropical fruit and locally grown root crops. Depending on the accommodation or village program, visitors may encounter dishes such as kokoda, raw fish marinated with citrus and coconut milk; rourou, taro leaves cooked with coconut milk; curries; grilled fish; and food prepared in a traditional underground lovo oven. \nSome village visits, cultural evenings and resort programs include communal meals, kava ceremonies or a \"lovo\" feast. These should be arranged through an accommodation provider or local guide rather than visiting a village unannounced. \nVisitors staying in self-catering accommodation should buy essential supplies in Vunisea or before flying or sailing to Kadavu. Shops outside the main settlement have limited stock, and imported foods may be expensive or temporarily unavailable. Bring any specialty foods, infant supplies or medically necessary dietary products from Suva or Nadi.\nPacked lunches can usually be arranged for diving, fishing, kayaking and hiking excursions. Carry additional water and snacks on longer outings, since there may be nowhere to purchase food once away from the resort or village.\nSleep.\nThe Island only has a handful of resorts to choose from Matava Resort, Tiliva Resort, Papageno Resort, Waisalima Beach Resort and Dive Center, Mai Dive Astrolabe Reef Resort and Nagigia Island Resort. The Matava Resort is located in the southern island group which is totally devoted to sustainable tourism. The Tiliva Resort is located near the Astrolabe and Solo Reefs which allow tourist to be closer to the beaches and water activities. Papageno Resort has 350 acres of land for their Resort and remote feel for guest, on Papageno guests are able to see the volcanic mountains and beaches. Waisalima Beach Resort and Dive Center offer a more aquatic experience and caters to the needs and wants of the guest. The Waisalima Beach Resort is remote, isolated and tranquil. Mai Dive Astrolabe Reef Resort is located beach front and is a colonial style living rather than traditional Fijian bures. The Mai offers a more modern feel to Kadavu than other Resorts. Lastly the Nagigia Island Resort (pronounced \"Na-Neen-Ya\"), Nagigia Island is named after the Senagigia flower that is unique to Nagigia Island. Nagigia offers 10 separate 'bures' (Fijian style cottages) poised over the waters edge with great sea views. Each of the resorts promote the same attraction and have similar activities. The accommodations for Kadavu are solely based on how the guest would like to experience the island and what of feel they would like to have in the Resort they are staying at.\nMatava.\nOn the remote and magnificent island of Kadavu, Matava - Fiji's Premier Eco Adventure Resort boasts secluded splendor which also satisfying ones craving for adventure and exploration. Bordered by the Great Astrolabe Reef Matava is a small and intimate getaway. Matava is designed to blend with the natural environment of Fiji and all it has to offer. Traditional Fijian bures are located in the Kadavu rainforest and are set around lush tropical surroundings. Matava accommodates up to 22 guests at one time and offer privacy, comfort and superb ocean views. Each guest will experience and amazing Kadavu experience which will be true to Fijian culture and customs. PADI Certified, Matava offers a unique diving experience and is located only a few minutes away from the Great Astrolabe Reef, Matava offers the perfect environment to scuba dive in Fiji, snorkel, surf, fish, kayak with opportunities to trek, visit villages, bird watch or simply relax and unwind.\nMatava is an eco-hideaway designed to cause minimal impact on the environment and to promote conservation awareness within the local community. Matava is the genuine eco-adventure lodge, beautifully set off the beaten track which allows for privacy and isolation. The intimate resort has beautiful, comfortable rooms, outstanding cuisine and offers a full range of adventure and cultural activities that makes it the premiere resort on Kadavu.\nMatava will cost you about the same as a five-star hotel. Your level of comfort will be similar to the Middle Ages. You will experience the absence of many basic services and necessities as part of the eco experience. Many basic comforts such as fan in your room, insect proofing bures, night lights, electricity after 5PM are missing at this resort. You are expected to be happy with cold showers, mud beaches and spiders in your room. After staying at Matava, I have developed a fear of the term \"eco\". It means poor service and having to put up with third world standards while paying exorbitant prices. If you ask for basic needs and comforts to be accommodated, you will probably be told that \"NO, we are an eco resort! We do not provide that.\" (e.g., powerpoints in your room, cold water to drink, proper coffee).\nThe diving here is good. However, the locals live off the reefs and hunt them regularly. The large fish have been speared and the remaining fish are wary of divers.\nOn a positive note, the food is very good, the Fijian staff are excellent, the views are great, the diving operation is well run.\nStay safe.\nSafety while on the island is totally provided by the resorts. Each bures has informational packet about safety tips for the island and ways to stay away from danger. These information session also preserves the fragile environment of Kadavu and gives visitors advice on how guests can avoid damaging the wildlife. Each of these programs are put in place for the safety of the visitor and to preserve the island. All safety regulations go along with the island's Responsible Tourism Code of Conduct.\nGo next.\nOno and Galoa are two other close destinations that are right off the coast of Kadavu and are easily accessible by boat."}
{"id": "16615", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=16615", "title": "Kadji Say", "text": "Kadji Sai (\u043a\u0430\u0436\u044b \u0441\u0430\u0439) is a small picturesque town in the middle of the south lakeshore of Issyk Kul in Kyrgyzstan.\nUnderstand.\nIt lies a few kilometres from the shore, where there is a bus stop. There is a long popular alley, an old building built by German war prisoners (actually the best-made building in town). Behind the town there is a stone wall.\nGet in.\nAll means of transport go by Kadji Sai along the highway between Karakol and Balykchy. A marshrutka from Bishkek costs around 350 soms (Sept 2009) and takes around 4 hr. Or head to Balykchy first and then take another transport towards Karakol along the southern shore.\nSee.\nBeautiful surroundings. There is also orphanage for ethnic Kyrgiz from all over the region of Central Asia. But most known is for its former surface uranium mine that is now practically deserted because of its low richness. It is not very secured, so when it rains (fortunately it is not often), radioactive water flows to the lake. But swimming there is excellent, with a bit of adventure. \nEat.\nThere is a cafe in and a shop.\nSleep.\nMost places can be found in its twin village \"Kadji-Sai 2\" at the highway, like:"}
